**Summary of what was authored:**
`courses/optyka-vs-sar-porownawcza-wizja-komputerowa/lessons/non-local-means-i-nowoczesne-despeckling.json`
- **Structure**: 6 sections in order theory → plotImage → theory → code → quiz → sandbox (2 theory + 4 widgets, no two theories back-to-back, ends on a widget).
- **Theory 1** (253 words): patch-similarity intuition with KaTeX formula and a parameter table; inline image of target+similar+dissimilar patches with quantified weights.
- **PlotImage**: NL-means weight map around one target pixel of the speckled cameraman, with axes/labels/ticks per the US-060 rules and the full reproducer source.
- **Theory 2** (289 words): why classical NL-means fails on multiplicative speckle, log-domain workaround, plus PPB / SAR-BM3D as the elegant-but-out-of-Pyodide alternatives. Inline 4-panel comparison (clean / speckled / Lee / NL-means) with concrete PSNR + ENL numbers.
- **Code**: implement `nl_means_sar(img, h, patch_size, patch_distance)` wrapping `skimage.restoration.denoise_nl_means` in the log domain. 4 tests (1 visible smoke test, 3 hidden) — all pass against the reference solution; ENL on cameraman sky grows 74× in the smoke run. Inputs and outputMedia point at locally-cached assets.
- **Quiz**: multi-select 4-option question (3 correct) wired to the misconceptions in `research.md`.
- **Sandbox**: tweakable `h` / `patch_size` / `patch_distance` with a hint to swap in plain `denoise_nl_means` to see the pre-log-domain failure mode.
- **Sources**: 6 lesson-level (Buades 2005, Deledalle 2009, Parrilli 2012, Argenti 2013, skimage docs, Oliver & Quegan book) + section-level on each theory and the code widget. All stable URLs.
